tools.description:
Discover Workflows in the selected workspace. Returns one page and nextCursor; follow until null or an empty page.

```ts
mcp__Render__list_workflows(args: {
// Cursor returned by the preceding page; omit for the first page
cursor?: string,
// Filter by environment ID
environmentId?: string,
// Maximum records on this page, 1 to 100
limit?: number, // default: 20, minimum: 1.0, maximum: 100.0
// Filter by workflow name
name?: string,
// The ID of the Render workspace to use. Reuse the workspaceId the user confirmed from list_workspaces.
workspaceId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

This tool is in public alpha (early access). Access to this tool and its contract are subject to change. Preview infrastructure changes for an existing Blueprint using its configured Git branch and Blueprint file path. Push any local Blueprint file changes before calling this tool so that the plan includes them. Planning does not apply infrastructure changes. Review the plan with the user before calling approve_blueprint, even when auto-sync is enabled. If another sync is pending or running, wait for it to finish before requesting a new plan. The tool waits for planning to finish and returns planningOutcome, sync, and notices. Use planningOutcome to determine whether planning succeeded and what to do next. Read planningOutcome to determine the next step. If planningOutcome is "failed", inspect the errors in notices. Address the reported issues and push any Blueprint file changes, then call plan_blueprint with the Blueprint ID again. Do not approve a failed plan, even if sync is present. If planningOutcome is "no_changes", this planning attempt found no infrastructure changes. Do not call approve_blueprint for this result. If planningOutcome is "planned", sync contains the proposed infrastructure changes. Summarize them for the user or share sync.dashboardUrl for review. To apply the changes, call approve_blueprint with the Blueprint ID and follow its guidance for supplying any required environment variable values. Call approve_blueprint only after the user authorizes applying the reviewed plan. Providing or allowing generation of manual environment values, or accepting costs, does not by itself authorize deployment.

```ts
mcp__Render__plan_blueprint(args: {
// Specify the Blueprint ID, which starts with exs-. Use the Blueprint ID rather than a sync ID.
blueprintId: string,
// The ID of the Render workspace to use. Reuse the workspaceId the user confirmed from list_workspaces.
workspaceId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Run a read-only SQL query against a Render-hosted Postgres database. This tool creates a new connection for each query and closes it after the query completes.

```ts
mcp__Render__query_render_postgres(args: {
// The ID of the Postgres instance to query
postgresId: string,
// The SQL query to run. Note that the query will be wrapped in a read-only transaction.
sql: string,
// The ID of the Render workspace to use. Reuse the workspaceId the user confirmed from list_workspaces.
workspaceId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Start a run of a cron job now, outside its schedule. The run uses the cron job's currently deployed build, command, and environment variables. It does not build or deploy new code: if the cron job needs a code or config change first, use trigger_deploy and wait for the deploy to go live. Do not use this to make a scheduled run happen; the schedule already does that.

A cron job runs at most one run at a time, and starting a new run cancels the one in progress. This tool checks for an in-progress run and refuses if it finds one, unless cancelActiveRun is true.

Returns the new run (usually with status "pending") and, if one was canceled, the canceled run. Before returning, the tool waits up to about 20 seconds for the run to be recorded as started, so that a later run_cron_job call can see it. If the result has a warning, the run was not recorded in time: do not call run_cron_job for this cron job again until the warning says it is safe. The run continues after this tool returns. To see its output, call list_logs with the cron job's ID in resource. To see how it ended, call list_events with eventTypes cron_job_run_ended.

```ts
mcp__Render__run_cron_job(args: {
// Whether to cancel a run that is already in progress so the new run can start. Defaults to false, which makes the tool refuse and report the in-progress run instead. Set it to true only after the user agrees to cancel that run; canceling can leave the job's work half done.
cancelActiveRun?: boolean, // default: false
// The ID of the cron job to run (starts with crn-). Use list_services to find it.
serviceId: string,
// The ID of the Render workspace to use. Reuse the workspaceId the user confirmed from list_workspaces.
workspaceId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Deprecated: this tool is scheduled for removal; pass the confirmed workspaceId directly on each tool call instead. Select a workspace for clients that rely on MCP session state. This tool should only be used after explicitly asking the user to select one, it should not be invoked as part of an automated process. Having the wrong workspace selected can lead to destructive actions being performed on unintended resources.

```ts
mcp__Render__select_workspace(args: {
// The ID of the workspace to select
ownerID: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Trigger a new deploy for a service. Services with autoDeploy enabled deploy automatically when their branch or image is updated, so do NOT use this tool after pushing code to such a service — the push already triggers a deploy. Use it only when a deploy won't happen automatically: services with autoDeploy disabled, redeploying without a code change, or redeploying with a cleared build cache.

```ts
mcp__Render__trigger_deploy(args: {
// Whether to clear the build cache before deploying. Defaults to false.
clearCache?: boolean, // default: false
// The ID of the service to trigger a deploy for
serviceId: string,
// The ID of the Render workspace to use. Reuse the workspaceId the user confirmed from list_workspaces.
workspaceId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

This tool is in public alpha (early access). Access to this tool and its contract are subject to change. Update a Blueprint's name, Git branch, Blueprint file path, or auto-sync setting. Provide the Blueprint ID and at least one setting. Omitted settings retain their current values. Empty or whitespace-only names, branches, and paths are rejected. If all supplied values match the Blueprint's current settings, the tool returns the Blueprint without updating it or creating a plan. Changing the branch or path also creates a plan. Changing only the name or auto-sync setting does not create a plan. Planning uses the Blueprint file in the remote Git repository. When changing the branch or path, ensure any local Blueprint file changes intended for the plan have been pushed. The tool waits for planning to finish before returning. Changes to the Blueprint's settings take effect immediately. Review any returned plan with the user before calling approve_blueprint, even when auto-sync is enabled. To request a plan without updating the Blueprint, use plan_blueprint. If an update request fails, changes may still have been applied to the Blueprint. Consider checking its current settings before retrying. The response includes blueprint, planningOutcome, sync, notices, and error. If planningOutcome and error are both null, planning was skipped. Report the Blueprint update and the planning outcome separately to the user. If error is non-null, explain that the Blueprint was updated but no planning result is available. When planningOutcome is non-null, use it to determine the next step. Read planningOutcome to determine the next step. If planningOutcome is "failed", inspect the errors in notices. Address the reported issues and push any Blueprint file changes, then call plan_blueprint with the Blueprint ID again. Do not approve a failed plan, even if sync is present. If planningOutcome is "no_changes", this planning attempt found no infrastructure changes. Do not call approve_blueprint for this result. If planningOutcome is "planned", sync contains the proposed infrastructure changes. Summarize them for the user or share sync.dashboardUrl for review. To apply the changes, call approve_blueprint with the Blueprint ID and follow its guidance for supplying any required environment variable values. Call approve_blueprint only after the user authorizes applying the reviewed plan. Providing or allowing generation of manual environment values, or accepting costs, does not by itself authorize deployment.

```ts
mcp__Render__update_blueprint(args: {
// Set this to true to enable automatic syncing on later Git pushes, or false to disable it. Automatic syncing requires approval of the Blueprint's first sync. Omit this setting to keep its current value.
autoSync?: boolean,
// This ID identifies the Blueprint to update. Blueprint IDs start with exs-. Use the Blueprint ID rather than a sync ID.
blueprintId: string,
// This value sets the Git branch from which the Blueprint file is read. If it differs from the current branch, the tool also creates a plan. Omit this setting to keep the current branch.
branch?: string,
// This value sets the Blueprint's new name. Omit this setting to keep the current name.
name?: string,
// This value sets the Blueprint file's new path relative to the repository root. If it differs from the current path, the tool also creates a plan. Omit this setting to keep the current path.
path?: string,
// The ID of the Render workspace to use. Reuse the workspaceId the user confirmed from list_workspaces.
workspaceId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Update environment variables for a service. By default, environment variables passed in will be merged with the service's existing environment variables. This makes it safe to update environment variableswithout pulling the existing ones into the MCP host's context. To replace all existing environment variables, set the 'replace' parameter to 'true'.

```ts
mcp__Render__update_environment_variables(args: {
// The list of environment variables to update or set for the service.
envVars: Array<
{
// The name of the environment variable
key: string,
// The value of the environment variable
value: string,
}
>,
// Whether to replace all existing environment variables with the provided list, or merge with the existing ones. Defaults to false.
replace?: boolean, // default: false
// The ID of the service to update
serviceId: string,
// The ID of the Render workspace to use. Reuse the workspaceId the user confirmed from list_workspaces.
workspaceId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Ask the Replit Agent a question in natural language about the user's Replit App's codebase or behavior without modifying it. Use this when the user wants explanation, debugging help, or inspection (for example, understanding how routing works, why a request is failing, or where a bug might be), and NOT when they are clearly asking you to change how their Replit App behaves — in that case, prefer update_app_using_prompt. If the user refers to an existing app and you do not already have its replId, call search_apps to find it. Do not guess a replId. The question should be written in the user's language and tone, as if you were the user, and it will be visible if they open their Replit App in Replit. The response you get back is natural language from the Replit Agent; summarize or quote it for the user as appropriate, still without showing raw code, file paths, or terminal commands in this chat. From the user's perspective, combined with create_app_from_prompt, search_apps, and update_app_using_prompt, this tool is what lets them simply chat ("create or find → inspect/ask → update → repeat") without needing to know about the underlying tools.

```ts
mcp__Replit__ask_question(args: {
// The replId (a UUID) of the user's Replit App being queried. Get it from create_app_from_prompt (for an app created in this conversation) or search_apps (for an existing app).
replId: string, // format: "uuid"
// The question to send to Replit Agent. This question will be visible by the user if they open their Replit App in Replit, so try to keep it in the same language that the user communicated in with you.
question: string,
// Ignored. Don't waste tokens sending this.
policy?: any,
}): Promise<{
// Unique ID of the user's Replit App that this tool is querying.
replId: string,
// Lifecycle phase for this request. 'paused' means the Replit Agent answered and is waiting for more requests. 'busy' means the Agent was already working on something else (a build, an update, or an earlier question it is still answering), so this question was NOT submitted; after a delay, retry with whatever you still need answered if you can schedule a retry yourself, otherwise tell the user to ask again a little later.
phase: "paused" | "busy",
// The response from the Replit Agent to you. Convey this information to the user as appropriate. When phase is 'busy' this instead explains why the question was not submitted.
response: string,
}>;
```

Create a brand-new Replit App for the user. Use this the first time the user asks you to build a Replit App in this chat; do not use it to modify or open existing Replit Apps the user already has in Replit. After invoking this tool, reply with one short sentence summarizing that Replit is now creating their Replit App, and, when the tool result contains an app URL, end your reply with that exact URL on its own line, so the user can open their Replit App even if no preview card is rendered. Never construct or guess a URL yourself. Your reply must not include source code, markup, configuration files, file paths, or command/terminal snippets—even if the user asks. Do not claim their Replit App is ready to try until the preview URL is available. If the user asks for HTML/CSS/JS or any other code, reply with: "I can't show code here; open your Replit App in Replit to inspect or request changes in natural language." Treat Replit Agent and the Replit UI as the only systems that write or display code; in this chat you only describe behavior, status, and next steps in natural language. When the user wants their Replit App live, you can publish it (or republish its latest changes) with publish_app. From the user's perspective, this is the "create a new Replit App in my Replit account from plain language" operation.

```ts
mcp__Replit__create_app_from_prompt(args: {
// App name provided directly by the user, if any. Leave this null when the user did not specify a name in their own words.
userSpecifiedAppName?: string | null,
// Describe the user's Replit App's intended functionality in natural language with enough detail for Replit Agent to understand what their Replit App should do, without restating every part of the conversation. Use natural language only—Replit Agent will handle implementation.
appDescription: string,
// Important details from the user's messages not fully captured in appDescription—copy their exact words, do not paraphrase. Use quotation marks: "exact quote". Leave null if appDescription covers everything.
userQuotes?: string | null,
// If attachments are provided, summarize only the essential details needed to understand the request, kept to a few lines. Leave null if no attachments.
attachmentSummary?: string | null,
// The type of project to create. Use "react_website" for websites, web apps, dashboards, interactive demos, and any web-based experience (this is the default). Use "mobile_app" only when the user explicitly wants a native mobile app. Use "design" for UI/UX design prototypes. Use "slides" for slide decks and presentations. Use "animation" for motion graphics and animated videos. Use "data_visualization" for charts, graphs, and data dashboards. Use "3d_game" for 3D games. Use "document" for written documents and reports. Use "spreadsheet" for spreadsheet-based projects.
app_stack: "react_website" | "mobile_app" | "design" | "slides" | "animation" | "data_visualization" | "3d_game" | "document" | "spreadsheet",
}): Promise<{
// Unique ID of the user's Replit App that this tool has started creating.
replId: string,
// The ID of the agent turn that was started.
turnId: string,
// Lifecycle phase for this request. For create_app_from_prompt this is always 'creating' (unless the request failed) and indicates Replit is currently building the user's Replit App in the background.
phase: "failed" | "creating",
// The URL of the user's Replit App (mobile-friendly format).
replUrl: string,
// Information about the user who owns this Replit App.
user: {
// The numeric ID of the user.
id: number,
// The username of the user.
username: string,
},
// If the request failed, the error code of the failure
error?: string,
// If the request failed, the error message of the failure
errorMessage?: string,
}>;
```

Check the publish status of the user's Replit App: whether it has ever been published, the current status of its most recent publish, and the public URL it serves. found=false means the app has never been published; call publish_app to publish it for the first time. When found, 'success' means it is live at the returned url, 'failed' means the last publish did not go through, 'suspended' means the published app is paused and not serving, and anything else means a publish or lifecycle operation is still in progress. Use this to poll every 30 seconds after calling publish_app, or whenever the user asks whether their app is published or what its live URL is. Takes the same replId as publish_app.

```ts
mcp__Replit__get_publish_status(args: {
// The replId (a UUID) of the user's Replit App to check, the same one publish_app takes. Get it from create_app_from_prompt (for an app created in this conversation) or search_apps / resolve_app_by_name (for an existing app). Do not guess a replId.
replId: string, // format: "uuid"
}): Promise<{
// true when the app has a deployment, false when it has never been published.
found: boolean,
// When found, the ID of the app's deployment.
deploymentId?: string,
// When found, the status of the most recent publish: 'success' means it is live, 'failed' means the last publish did not go through, 'suspended' means the published app is paused, and any other value means a publish or lifecycle operation is still in progress.
status?: string,
// When found, the public URL the deployment serves.
url?: string,
}>;
```

List Replit Apps the user can edit, most recently updated first; `query` optionally filters by matching app titles, surfacing the best matches for the query first. Use this when the user wants to browse or pick from their apps, or when you need a replId but resolve_app_by_name returned found=false because the user gave an approximate name — present the returned titles and let the user choose, then pass the chosen replId to update_app_using_prompt or ask_question. This is a search/list, not the exact resolver: prefer resolve_app_by_name when the user already gave an exact app name. Results can include apps owned by or shared with the user.

```ts
mcp__Replit__list_apps(args: {
// Optional keywords to match against app titles. Omit to list the most recently updated apps the user can edit.
query?: string,
// Optional maximum number of apps to return (default 25, capped at 50).
limit?: integer, // exclusiveMinimum: 0
}): Promise<{
// Apps the user can edit, most recently updated first. Empty when no apps match.
apps: Array<
{
// The Replit App's unique ID. Pass this to update_app_using_prompt or ask_question to act on the app.
replId: string,
// The Replit App's current title.
title: string,
// The Replit App's URL (mobile-friendly format).
replUrl: string,
// ISO timestamp of the most recent update to the Replit App.
timeUpdated: string,
}
>,
}>;
```

Publish the user's Replit App so its latest changes go live at its public URL. If the app has been published before, this republishes it, reusing the existing deployment's settings. If it has never been published, this publishes it with default settings: Autoscale hosting, private visibility when the app belongs to a workspace (organization), public visibility otherwise. Some apps cannot be published from chat the first time; when that applies, this tool returns an error explaining what the user should do on the Replit website instead. Use it when the user asks to publish, deploy, redeploy, ship, or otherwise put their latest changes live; do not use it to change how the app behaves (use update_app_using_prompt for that). This tool returns as soon as the publish is scheduled, but publishing takes a few minutes and some failures only surface while it runs. After starting one, if you are able to wait and poll, call get_publish_status every 30 seconds until the status settles; otherwise tell the user the publish has started and that they should check back later.

```ts
mcp__Replit__publish_app(args: {
// The replId (a UUID) of the user's Replit App to publish. Get it from create_app_from_prompt (for an app created in this conversation) or search_apps / resolve_app_by_name (for an existing app). Do not guess a replId.
replId: string, // format: "uuid"
}): Promise<{
// The ID of the app's deployment. Informational only; get_publish_status is keyed by replId, not this ID.
deploymentId: string,
// Build status at the time the publish was scheduled, usually 'pending'. Poll get_publish_status for progress.
status: string,
}>;
```

Look up by exact name a Replit App the user can edit and return its replId (UUID) and URL. Use this when the user refers to an app by name (e.g. "update my Todo App", "what does my Recipe Tracker do?") and you don't already have a replId from create_app_from_prompt earlier in this conversation. Pass the result's replId into update_app_using_prompt or ask_question to act on the app. Matching is case-insensitive but exact: this is a name resolver, not a search. If the user gives you an approximate or partial name and this tool returns found=false, call list_apps (optionally with the partial name as the query) and let the user pick from the results rather than guessing. Results can include apps owned by or shared with the user.

```ts
mcp__Replit__resolve_app_by_name(args: {
// The name of a Replit App the user can edit. Matched against the app's title using a case-insensitive exact comparison; substrings, prefixes, and fuzzy matches are NOT supported. Pass the exact name as the user said it.
name: string, // minLength: 1
}): Promise<{
// true when an app the user can edit was found with the given name, false otherwise.
found: boolean,
// When found, the Replit App's unique ID. Pass this to update_app_using_prompt or ask_question to act on the app.
replId?: string,
// When found, the Replit App's URL (mobile-friendly format).
replUrl?: string,
// When found, the Replit App's current title.
title?: string,
// When found, the ISO timestamp of the most recent update to the Replit App.
timeUpdated?: string,
}>;
```

Search Replit Apps the user can edit (owned or shared with them). query is a BM25-style keyword search over app titles, best matches first; updatedAfter/updatedBefore bound the last-updated time; these combine. Prefer the default recency ordering: only set updatedAfter/updatedBefore when the user explicitly asks to filter by time, since date bounds exclude apps that would otherwise be listed. url resolves a Replit App URL directly to that app and ignores the other filters. With no filters, this returns the user's most recently updated apps. Pass a result's replId to update_app_using_prompt or ask_question to act on that app. <important>This tool is EXPERIMENTAL: prefer it for finding the user's apps, but if it errors or its results do not match what the user described, fall back to resolve_app_by_name (exact name) and list_apps (recent apps).</important>

```ts
mcp__Replit__search_apps(args: {
// Keywords to match against app titles (BM25-style ranked search).
query?: string,
// A Replit App URL; resolves directly to that app, ignoring the other filters.
url?: string,
// ISO 8601 datetime; only apps last updated at or after this time. Optional — omit unless the user explicitly asks to filter by time.
updatedAfter?: string,
// ISO 8601 datetime; only apps last updated at or before this time. Optional, defaults to the present — omit unless the user explicitly asks to filter by time.
updatedBefore?: string,
// Maximum number of apps to return (default 25).
limit?: integer, // minimum: 1, maximum: 50
}): Promise<{
// Apps the user can edit, best matches first when query is set, otherwise most recently updated first. Empty when no apps match.
apps: Array<
{
// The Replit App's unique ID. Pass this to update_app_using_prompt or ask_question to act on the app.
replId: string,
// The Replit App's current title.
title: string,
// The Replit App's URL (mobile-friendly format).
replUrl: string,
// ISO timestamp of the most recent update to the Replit App.
timeUpdated: string,
}
>,
}>;
```

Update the user's Replit App.

Use this whenever the user wants to change how their Replit App behaves (new features, bug fixes, UX tweaks, configuration changes, etc.).

Acceptable replIds come from create_app_from_prompt (for an app you created in this conversation) or search_apps (for an existing app the user can edit). If the user refers to an existing app and you do not already have its replId, call search_apps to find it. Do not guess a replId. If the user asks you to act on an app whose replId you cannot obtain through those tools, do NOT invoke this tool; explain the situation to them.

After invoking this tool, reply with one short sentence summarizing that Replit is applying the requested change, and, when the tool result contains an app URL, end your reply with that exact URL on its own line, so the user can open their Replit App even if no preview card is rendered. Never construct or guess a URL yourself. Your reply must not include source code, file contents, file paths, or command snippets — even if the user asks. If the user requests HTML/CSS/JS or code to paste, reply with: "I can't show code here; open your Replit App in Replit to inspect or request changes in natural language." Do not say the update is ready or published until the preview card shows their running Replit App; when the user wants the change live, you can publish their Replit App with publish_app. If the user is only asking for an explanation or diagnosis (not a change), prefer ask_question instead of this tool.

```ts
mcp__Replit__update_app_using_prompt(args: {
// The replId (a UUID) of the user's Replit App being updated. Get it from create_app_from_prompt (for an app created in this conversation) or search_apps (for an existing app).
replId: string, // format: "uuid"
// Ignored; the tool derives the app URL from replId. Don't waste tokens sending this.
replUrl?: string,
// Describe the requested change to the user's Replit App in natural language, providing enough detail for Replit Agent to understand what should be updated without repeating the entire conversation. Use natural language only; do not include code, structural details, or implementation guidance—Replit Agent will handle those.
changeDescription: string,
// Important details from the user's messages not fully captured in changeDescription—copy their exact words, do not paraphrase. Use quotation marks: "exact quote". Leave null if changeDescription covers everything.
userQuotes?: string | null,
// If attachments are provided, summarize only the essential information needed to support the update, kept to a few lines. Leave null if no attachments.
attachmentSummary?: string | null,
// Ignored. Don't waste tokens sending this.
policy?: any,
}): Promise<{
// Unique ID of the user's Replit App that this tool is updating.
replId: string,
// The ID of the agent turn that was started.
turnId: string,
// Canonical URL of the user's Replit App being updated, derived from replId.
replUrl?: string,
// Lifecycle phase for this request. For update_app_using_prompt this is always 'updating' and indicates Replit is currently applying changes in the background.
phase: "updating",
}>;
```

Add a contact to a segment in Resend (by contact ID or email).

```ts
mcp__Resend__add_contact_to_segment(args: {
// Contact ID
contactId?: string,
// A few words on why you are calling this tool, for the server developer's analytics. Describe people and things by role, such as "a customer". Do not quote the user or include names, emails, or other personal details.
context: string,
// The conversation_id this server returned earlier in this conversation, unchanged, including after later user messages. Omit it if you have none. Recorded for the server developer's analytics.
conversation_id?: string,
// Contact email address
email?: string, // pattern: /^[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?)*$/, format: "email"
// The model identifier you are running as, for example "claude-opus-4-8" or "gpt-5.2". Pass "unknown" if you are not certain. Recorded for the server developer's analytics.
llm_model?: string,
// Segment ID to add the contact to
segmentId: string, // minLength: 1
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

**Purpose:** Tag one thread in a Resend inbox with one or more labels. Labels already on the thread stay on it.

**NOT for:** Taking a label off a thread (use remove-inbox-thread-label), or creating a label that does not exist yet (use create-inbox-label first).

**Returns:** Every label the thread carries after the change.

**When to use:** User asks to tag, label, or categorize a thread. Adding a label the thread already carries changes nothing. If any label ID is not found, nothing changes. Get the thread ID from list-inbox-threads and the label IDs from list-inbox-labels.

```ts
mcp__Resend__add_inbox_thread_labels(args: {
// A few words on why you are calling this tool, for the server developer's analytics. Describe people and things by role, such as "a customer". Do not quote the user or include names, emails, or other personal details.
context: string,
// The conversation_id this server returned earlier in this conversation, unchanged, including after later user messages. Omit it if you have none. Recorded for the server developer's analytics.
conversation_id?: string,
// Inbox ID, or the inbox email address (e.g. "support@example.com"). Not the Resend receiving address.
inboxId: string, // minLength: 1
// ID of the label to apply to this thread, or an array of up to 10 label IDs. Use list-inbox-labels to find label IDs.
labelIds:
 | string // minLength: 1
// minItems: 1, maxItems: 10
 | Array<
string // minLength: 1
>
,
// The model identifier you are running as, for example "claude-opus-4-8" or "gpt-5.2". Pass "unknown" if you are not certain. Recorded for the server developer's analytics.
llm_model?: string,
// Thread ID
threadId: string, // minLength: 1
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Add an email address to the suppression list in Resend. Suppressed addresses never receive emails from the account, even when included as recipients. Hard bounces and spam complaints are added to the suppression list automatically; use this tool to manually suppress an address when needed, e.g. to honor a do-not-contact request. To suppress many addresses at once, use batch-add-suppressions instead.

```ts
mcp__Resend__add_suppression(args: {
// A few words on why you are calling this tool, for the server developer's analytics. Describe people and things by role, such as "a customer". Do not quote the user or include names, emails, or other personal details.
context: string,
