Search SciSpace's index of 280M+ scholarly papers across all disciplines. Returns JSON results with `id`, `title`, `text` and `url`; `text` holds the year, authors, venue, citation count, TL;DR and an abstract excerpt.

Use this when the user asks for research papers, studies, literature, evidence or citations on a topic, or when an answer should be grounded in scholarly sources. Write `query` as a full natural-language research question ("How does sleep deprivation affect memory consolidation?"), not keywords; if the user gave keywords, rewrite them into a question first. To find a kind of paper, such as systematic reviews or meta-analyses, say so in the question.

To request filters, include tokens in the query text: `year:2020-2026` (also `year:2021`, `year:2018-`, `year:-2015`), `citations:50` (cited at least 50 times), `oa:true` (open access only), `pdf:true` (full text on SciSpace), `top:true` (top journals and conferences only), `sort:most_cited` (or newest, oldest, relevance), `limit:50` (default 20, max 100). Keep years and venues out of the question itself.

Citing: cite a paper with its `url`, the paper's SciSpace page, not a DOI or a publisher link. The search result is enough to cite a paper; call `fetch` only when you need more than its excerpt.

Showing results: this tool displays nothing to the user. When the user asked for papers and you ran one search, call `show_table` straight after with the `id`s of its results and the query, so they see the papers in an interactive table. When you are running several searches to research a question, keep searching without showing each one, then call `show_table` once at the end with the ids of the papers worth showing - or skip it when the user only wanted an answer.

Each `id` also works with `extract_data` (methods, results, limitations or any custom field).

